\f1\fs22 \'91Now that we\'92ve put the kids to bed \'85\'92 Dunhilton began, then noticed Moral Observance\'92s hand spidering tentatively across the table towards the pastry plate. Dunhilton\'92s cold patrician stare froze the plump thing for a tense moment or two. It scurried into Moral Observance\'92s lap. \'91Let\'92s move on to Item Two of the agenda \'85 Ah! Item Three, now: The Elvi.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All the directors present smiled grimly now the meeting was getting down to business. Dunhilton could see them rehearsing their craven, self-interested proposals in their heads; he\'92d heard them all before. Corrections was in the pocket of the Aged Care industry  who wanted to build and run prisons; all Corrections needed to supply was a fresh supply of criminals ripe for incarceration. Housing wanted denser property development over the Ghetto (for which she would be handsomely bribed). Tourism wanted to drive the Elvi out of the Ghetto entirely and build a new tourist park, 
\f2\i Elvi World
\f1\i0 . Defence wanted to conscript the Elvi so he could invade Indonesia with a view to seizing every palm oil and durian plantation in South East Asia.  These were all excellent ideas, the CEO conceded, which together could in the fullness of time yield a fully integrated master plan of synergistic opportunities going forward. He just hated the idea of any of these grunts profiting from any of it. Besides, if he could take the Scotch out of Scotland, he could certainly take the venture capital wind from the sails of their nascent exploitations.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I still wonder why we ever emancipated them,\'92 Tourism said, despising the costs to the tourism industry of having to pay minimum wage once the Elvi were freed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What was Grayling Corporation\'92s slogan again?\'92 Housing mused.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i Get an ELVIS. It will thank you very much
\f1\i0 ,\'92 Tourism quoted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, the very first one.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah! I remember!\'92 Housing snapped his fingers to recall, then with much excitement, recited, \'91ELVIS. Putting the 
\f2\i slave
\f1\i0  back in 
\f2\i slavery
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, yes, that was it. I don\'92t understand why the marketing boffins at Grayling retired that one.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Moral observance spoke through the crumbs on his lips. \'91It\'92s apparent the Elvi pay no regard for the one true religion. I doubt any of them know the meaning of a balance sheet or a profit statement. And I am most concerned by this Holy-Momma-worship the Elvi are indulging in.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry diplomatically coughed, and glanced at the CEO. Neither wanted to discuss their virus project in a wider cabinet meeting. \'91There are intelligence efforts currently in progress regarding the Holy Momma.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I trust you have the initiatives well in hand Gerry,\'92 Dunhilton prompted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed, CEO, I have,\'92 Gerry answered, \'91if I can have a minute or two after cabinet.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Very well, let\'92s move on,\'92 Dunhilton declared over Moral Observance\'92s objections. Dunhilton checked himself and shook his head in frustration. \'91How could those decommissioned entertainment grunts gain access to the grounds of St Hedges anyway? The hover pad security scanners should have caught every last one of them and blown them to cinders before they\'92d set a single toe on black grass or hallowed gold pavement.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Planes,\'92 Gerry said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Planes?\'92 Defence repeated as if Gerry was demented. \'91There\'92s nowhere to land.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They used parachutes,\'92 Gerry answered. \'91Apparently, the Elvi know how to sky-dive.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Where would they learn such an ancient skill?\'92 Defence persisted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry shrugged. \'91We think the original Grayling cloning technology was capable of encoding certain experiences and skills into the base DNA. For example, it\'92s long been thought the Elvi could be masterful actors.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s preposterous.\'92 Moral Observance brushed the florid sleeves of his jacket with his pink cherubic hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Never mind whether they can sky-dive,\'92 Dunhilton said, becoming frustrating with the farce of it. \'91Where did they get a taxing plane?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Planes,\'92 Gerry corrected. \'91Probably a garbage tip somewhere. Or multiple tips. There\'92s all kinds of old tech underneath the banana skins, if you know where to look.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton snare Defence in his stare. \'91You must have seen a bunch of decrepit aircraft approaching St Hedge\'92s,\'92 he accused.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Defence seemed to collapse from the inside out, like a deflating airbed. \'91The Air Force saw them, but couldn\'92t catch them.\'92 The room fell as silent as witnesses to an impending execution, until Defence felt compelled to add, \'91The hover-copt fans of even our most modern gunships are no match for a fixed-wing aircraft.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton kept blinking away the impossibility of an underclass of cloned service grunts making a national defence force look so foolish. He considered handing the Defence portfolio to Gerry as well. But Gerry already had enough power, even if Dunhilton could taketh away even as only that morning he had giveneth.
